      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We know that $$\\tan x = 2 \\implies \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = 2,$$ so we can write $\\sin x$ in terms of $\\cos x$: $$\\sin x = 2 \\cos x.$$\n\nTo find $\\tan \\left( x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)$, we can use the tangent addition formula: \\[\n\\tan \\left( x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\tan x + \\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4}}{1 - \\tan x \\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4}}.\n\\] Knowing that $\\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4} = 1$, we substitute $\\tan x = 2$ and $\\tan \\frac{\\pi}{4} = 1$ into the formula: \\[\n\\tan \\left( x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\frac{2 + 1}{1 - 2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{3}{1 - 2} = \\frac{3}{-1} = \\boxed{-3}.\n\\]\n\nThe answer is: -3",
